| 17p5-15deg-400s-NoN2-1800s | 400.76 | **0.40** | minor fold |
| 17p5-15deg-200s-NoN2-1800s | 367.39 | **0.38** | clean ✓ |
| 19-10deg-240s-NoN2-1600s (run2) | 308.88 | **0.37** | minor wrinkles |

**Approximate Pareto front** (non-dominated in modulus × pore fraction):
- High modulus / lower pore fraction: ~936 bar / 0.27 (run1, single specimen, high CV, wrinkles)
- Balanced: ~658 bar / 0.31 (run6, clean, 1 independent repeat)
- High pore fraction / lower modulus: ~421 bar / 0.44 (run3, minor wrinkles, 5 runs of similar condition)

### Validation Status

- **17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run1)**: Modulus 936 bar is a large apparent gain, single specimen with SD=0 (only 1 fit survived), high uncertainty. Runs 2 and 6 gave 675 and 659 bar respectively — the 936 is likely an outlier. Not confirmed.
- **17p5-19deg-90s-N2-1800s (run1)**: 860 bar / 0.28, single specimen SD=0, wrinkle defect. Run2 gave 549 bar / 0.30. Not confirmed at 860.
- **18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s**: 5 runs with pore fraction 0.25–0.44, modulus 340–476 bar. High variability in pore fraction across runs. The run3 value of 0.44 is the highest observed pore fraction with a measured modulus, but the condition is variable.

### Key Observations

The **NoN2 + longer wait time** conditions (17p5-15deg-200s-NoN2, 17p5-15deg-400s-NoN2, 18-15deg-600s-NoN2) consistently produce **high pore fractions (0.38–0.44)** at the cost of lower modulus (~370–470 bar). The **N2 + bath_temp ~18-19°C** conditions produce **higher modulus (~600-860 bar)** but lower pore fraction (~0.27-0.31).

### Gap to Explore

The **NoN2 + intermediate wait time** region at **polymer_wt ~17.5, bath_temp ~15°C** has been explored at 200s and 400s wait times. The 200s condition gave a clean membrane with 367 bar / 0.38, and 400s gave 401 bar / 0.40 with a minor fold. 

An **unexplored region** that could push toward a better Pareto trade-off: **NoN2 + bath_temp ~12-13°C** (colder bath) with **polymer_wt ~17.5** and **moderate wait time ~150s**. Colder bath accelerates phase separation, which could increase modulus while the NoN2 + moderate wait time may preserve elevated pore fraction. This combination is not in the dataset.

Additionally, **pullcast_speed** has been nearly constant at 10 mm/s for most recent experiments. A **lower pullcast speed (~6-7 mm/s)** with the NoN2 + cold bath combination could reduce wrinkling tendency while probing a new region.

**Proposed experiment**: polymer_wt=17.5, bath_temp=10°C, mixing_temp=65°C, pullcast_speed=7, nitrogen=false, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=150s, nips_bath_wait_time=1800s.

**Expected benefit**: The colder bath (10°C vs 15°C) should increase phase separation rate, potentially raising modulus above the ~370-400 bar seen at 15°C NoN2 conditions. The NoN2 + 150s wait time may preserve elevated pore fraction (targeting >0.35). This is a genuinely new combination (bath_temp=10, NoN2, wait=150s, polymer=17.5 not previously run) that could improve the Pareto frontier by finding a point with both higher modulus and maintained high pore fraction compared to the 15°C NoN2 results.

