  "pullcast_speed": 10,
  "nitrogen": true,
  "coupon_to_bath_wait_time": 90,
  "nips_bath_wait_time": 1800,
  "polymer_wt": 18.0,
  "additive_wt": 0.0
}
```

**Rationale:**

- **Objective benefit:** The 18.5wt/12°C/120s/N2 experiments showed elevated pore fraction (0.37–0.38) alongside modulus in the 380–610 bar range. Shifting to 18.0wt (slightly lower concentration) at the same bath temperature may reduce wrinkling tendency (lower-concentration solutions are less viscous and may cast more smoothly) while retaining the elevated pore fraction driven by the 12°C bath. The 18wt/14°C/90s/N2 run gave 475 bar / 0.34 PF (clean membrane), suggesting 18wt at slightly colder bath could push PF higher. This point targets the balanced interior of the Pareto front (~500–650 bar modulus, ~0.35–0.40 PF).

- **What it teaches:** Whether 18wt at 12°C bath (vs. 14°C) increases pore fraction without sacrificing modulus, and whether the wrinkling seen at 18.5wt/12°C is concentration-driven or bath-temperature-driven. This is a genuinely new parameter combination (18.0wt + 12°C bath not previously sampled).

- **Diversity:** Adds a new (polymer_wt, bath_temp) combination not in the history, while keeping other parameters in the well-performing regime (mixing_temp=65, N2=true, pullcast_speed=10, short wait).

