[2026-11-03 06:29:05] LLM suggestion: ## Analysis

### Pareto-Front Candidates (both objectives measured)

Reviewing all experiments with both Elastic Modulus and Pore Fraction measured:

| Condition | Modulus (bar) | Pore Fraction | Quality |
|---|---|---|---|
| 17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run1) | 936.62 | 0.27 | minor wrinkles |
| 17p5-19deg-90s-N2-1800s (run1) | 860.94 | 0.28 | wrinkle |
| 17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run2) | 675.14 | 0.30 | wrinkle+gap |
| 17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run6) | 658.85 | 0.31 | clean ✓ |
| 18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s (run3) | 421.45 | 0.44 | minor wrinkles |
| 18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s (run5) | 468.22 | 0.40 | wrinkles |
| 17p5-15deg-200s-NoN2-1800s | 367.39 | 0.38 | clean ✓ |
| 17p5-15deg-400s-NoN2-1800s | 400.76 | 0.40 | minor fold |

The approximate Pareto front (maximizing both): high-modulus end is anchored by the 17.5wt%/18°C/75s/N2 cluster; high-pore-fraction end by the 18wt%/15°C/600s/NoN2 cluster. There's a clear tradeoff: higher bath temp + N2 + short wait → higher modulus, lower pore fraction; lower bath temp + no N2 + long wait → higher pore fraction, lower modulus.

### Validation Status

- **17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s**: run1 (936 bar, 0.27 PF) is a single measurement with SD=0 (only 1 specimen survived fitting), very high apparent modulus. Run2 gave 675 bar, 0.30 PF. Run6 gave 659 bar, 0.31 PF. The run1 value of 936 bar is likely an outlier; runs 2 and 6 are more consistent (~660-675 bar, 0.30-0.31 PF). Run6 had clean membrane quality. This candidate has 3 independent runs — the mean modulus is ~757 bar but with high variance driven by run1. The run2/run6 pair is more reproducible (~667 bar, 0.305 PF).

- **17p5-19deg-90s-N2-1800s**: run1 (861 bar, 0.28 PF) — single surviving fit, SD=0. Run2 gave 549 bar, 0.30 PF. Run3 gave 519 bar, 0.36 PF. Run4 gave 673 bar, 0.27 PF. High variability across runs; run1 likely outlier.

- **18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s**: 5 runs with modulus ranging 340–476 bar, pore fraction 0.32–0.44. High pore fraction is reproducible but modulus is moderate and variable.

### Key Observations

The high-pore-fraction region (0.38–0.44) is achieved with **no N2, longer wait times, bath temp ~15°C**, but at the cost of lower modulus (~370–470 bar). The high-modulus region (>600 bar) tends to have pore fraction ~0.27–0.31.

**Gap in the Pareto front**: Is there a combination that achieves both moderate-high modulus (>500 bar) AND moderate-high pore fraction (>0.35)? The 18p5-12deg-120s-N2-1800s (run1) showed modulus 611 bar, pore fraction 0.37 — but with very high CV (0.36) and significant wrinkling. Run2 gave 382 bar, 0.38 PF. This region (polymer_wt ~18–18.5, bath_temp ~11–13°C, N2, moderate wait) is promising but noisy.

The **18-12deg-90s-N2-1800s** experiment gave 593 bar modulus, 0.31 PF with clean membrane and low CV (0.18). This is a good quality result but pore fraction is modest.

**Under-explored region**: Higher polymer concentration (18–18.5 wt%) with bath temp ~11–13°C, N2, moderate wait (~90–120s) could potentially yield both higher modulus and higher pore fraction. The 18.5wt%/12°C/120s/N2 pair showed promise for pore fraction (0.37–0.38) but inconsistent modulus. 

**New exploration target**: Try **polymer_wt = 18.0, bath_temp = 11°C, coupon_to_bath_wait_time = 90s, N2 = true, pullcast_speed = 10, mixing_temp = 65, nips_bath_wait_time = 1800**. Bath temp 11°C is between the 10°C (lower PF ~0.29-0.31) and 12°C (higher PF ~0.31-0.38) results for 17-18 wt% polymer. This probes whether the pore fraction increase seen at 12°C can be achieved with better modulus stability at 18 wt% polymer. This is a genuinely new point (bath_temp=11 with polymer_wt=18 has not been tested) that could clarify the bath_temp/pore_fraction relationship in a promising region.

