- **18p5-12deg-120s-N2-1800s**: run1 gave (611, 0.37) with high CV=0.36 and wrinkles; run2 gave (382, 0.38) — very different modulus, suggesting high synthesis variability. Not confirmed.
- **18-12deg-90s-N2-1800s**: run1 gave (593, 0.31) clean membrane; run2 gave (349, 0.47) with wrinkles/gap. These are dramatically different — likely the run2 result is compromised by defects. The clean run1 is a strong candidate.

The **18-12deg-90s-N2-1800s** condition (polymer_wt=18, bath_temp=12, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=90, N2=true, nips_bath_wait_time=1800, mixing_temp=65, pullcast_speed=10) produced a clean membrane in run1 with modulus=593 and pore fraction=0.31. Run2 had severe defects and very different values. This condition needs a clean independent repeat to confirm whether the run1 result is reproducible.

However, looking more carefully at the Pareto landscape: the **18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s** series (runs 1–5) consistently shows pore fractions of 0.32–0.44 with moduli 340–476. Run3 (421, 0.44) is the best pore fraction with decent modulus among clean-ish membranes. But this condition has been run 5 times already with high variability.

### Exploration Opportunity

The data shows a clear pattern: **lower bath temperature** (5–12°C) with **polymer_wt ~17.5–18.5** and **N2=true** tends to give higher modulus. **Higher bath temperature** (15–20°C) or **no N2** with longer wait times tends to give higher pore fraction. 

An under-explored region that could push both objectives: **polymer_wt ~18–18.5, bath_temp ~10–12°C, N2=true, moderate wait time ~90–120s** — this is the zone where 18-12deg-90s-N2-1800s run1 showed promise (593, 0.31). But we need a clean repeat.

Actually, checking: **18-12deg-90s-N2-1800s** has run1 (clean, 593/0.31) and run2 (defective, 349/0.47). The policy requires validation when a candidate has not been independently reproduced with clean quality. Run1 was clean; run2 was defective. We need a third clean run to confirm run1's performance.

